1. Primary Cross-Border Legal Antinomies
A. Statutory QFC Stays vs. Foreign Close-Out Netting Rights
Under 12 U.S.C. § 5390(c)(8)–(16), the FDIC imposes an immediate temporary stay (typically ending at 5:00 PM ET on the business day following appointment) prohibiting counterparties from terminating Qualified Financial Contracts (QFCs) or seizing pledged collateral.

The Conflict: An EU-domiciled clearing member operating under home-country law (e.g., German KWG or French Monetary and Financial Code) may hold master agreements where statutory stays without an explicit contractual submission clause are unenforceable.


The Breach: If the foreign clearing member exercises immediate bilateral close-out netting under local law, it unilaterally severs its portfolio from the Bridge CCP, triggering uncontrolled localized market-making shocks and draining clearing liquidity.


B. Asset Ring-Fencing and Collateral Location (Lex Rei Sitae)
Let the collateral pool of the defaulting SIFMU be partitioned across domestic and foreign jurisdictions:


Ctotal=CUS+Cforeign
Where Cforeign includes sovereign bonds held via Euroclear/Clearstream, cash accounts in TARGET2, or local central bank deposits.

The Conflict: Under Title II, the FDIC orders a universal transfer of all clearing assets to the Bridge Financial Company (BCCP). However, foreign property transfers are governed by the principle of lex rei sitae (the law of the place where property is situated).


The Barrier: Foreign courts and National Competent Authorities (NCAs) under CCPRRR Article 75 may refuse to recognize the transfer if doing so prejudices domestic creditors or violates EMIR Article 39 (segregation requirements for Individual/Omnibus Accounts). Foreign custodians can freeze Cforeign, depriving the Bridge CCP of the liquidity required to meet settlement cycles.


C. Loss of EMIR Article 25 Third-Country Equivalence & QCCP Cliff
Under Regulation (EU) No 575/2013 (Capital Requirements Regulation / CRR) and EMIR:

Qualifying CCP (QCCP) Status: EU banks clearing through a recognized Tier 2 Third-Country CCP (TC-CCP) maintain favorable capital risk-weights ($\approx 2\%$).


The Bridge Discontinuity: When the FDIC establishes a Bridge Financial Company, BCCP is legally a new corporate entity possessing a temporary domestic charter. It lacks pre-existing recognition by the European Securities and Markets Authority (ESMA) under EMIR Article 25.


The Capital Shock: If ESMA does not grant emergency provisional equivalence, the Bridge CCP is classified as a non-qualifying CCP. EU clearing members face an immediate punitive capital risk-weight surge to $1250\%$ on all margin and default fund exposures, legally compelling EU entities to terminate membership and liquidate positions.


D. "No Creditor Worse Off" (NCWO) Valuation Discrepancies
Both Dodd-Frank Title II (12 U.S.C. § 5390(d)(2)) and CCPRRR Article 60 enforce the No Creditor Worse Off than under Normal Insolvency Proceedings (NCWO) safeguard:


