4. CPMI-IOSCO / FSB Recognition Mechanisms & Persistent Bottlenecks
To bridge the gap between territorial sovereignty and global clearing, standard-setting bodies deploy two main coordination mechanisms:

Crisis Management Groups (CMGs) & Cooperation Agreements: Under FSB Key Attribute 7, global SIFMU CCPs maintain CMGs comprising the Fed, SEC, CFTC, FDIC, ECB, ESMA, and the Bank of England. However, these Memoranda of Understanding (MoUs) are non-legally binding and are routinely superseded by domestic statutory obligations during systemic crises.


Contractual Recognition Clauses: Clearinghouses increasingly embed mandatory contractual recognition clauses into their rulebooks (compelling members to submit contractually to home-resolution stays). While effective against clearing members, this does not prevent sovereign foreign courts from issuing injunctions or national insolvency freezes on assets situated within their territorial jurisdiction.


