5. Asymmetric Reflexivity Under Systemic Bank Distress
When a G-SIB holding company enters distress, both frameworks interact with opposing systemic consequences:

DIF Procyclical Drag: As the bank's CAMELS rating deteriorates, its DIF scorecard performance score spikes, immediately increasing its quarterly IBAR rate RiDIF. This drains operating liquidity from the bank prior to resolution.


OLF Post-Resolution Ring-Fencing: If the parent BHC fails and is resolved under Title II, its operating bank IDI is shielded from the OLF clawback. The surviving peer institutions pay the OLF assessment at the parent BHC level, ensuring that the resolution of non-bank systemic clearing or broker-dealer operations does not directly impair depository-level capital reserves.


