1. Conceptual & Legal Architecture of the Single Obligation Structure (SOS)
In traditional gross settlement or periodic batch netting, payment orders create independent claims. If an insolvency occurs, an insolvency administrator could attempt to cherry-pick performing incoming transfers while rejecting outgoing claims (the cherry-picking risk, e.g., under § 103 of the German Insolvency Code (Insolvenzordnung — InsO)).

Under the Single Obligation Structure (SOS), governed by German law under the rules of EBA Clearing (EURO1), individual payment orders do not create independent claims or liabilities. Instead, the submission and acceptance of a payment order constitutes an immediate, irrevocable modification of a single, unitary continuous net obligation (laufende Rechnung pursuant to § 355 of the German Commercial Code (Handelsgesetzbuch — HGB) combined with continuous contractual novation / Schuldumwandlung).

